==German==
===Etymology===
From [[Middle High German]] -heid, which means 'State'<ref>The Grammar of Words: An Introduction to Linguistic Morphology
By Geert Booij page 296 (2016)</ref> <ref>The Oxford Handbook of Derivational Morphology
edited by Rochelle Lieber, Pavol Stekauer page 29</ref> The ending -heit comes from the Germanic word * haidu-, which became heid/heit in Old High German and could serve as a translation for the Latin words ''persona'' and ''sexus'' in the 8th and 9th century. In Middle High German heid/heit is rarely documented as an independent word and reduced to the meaning "way", "quality". As a suffix, it appeared very early next to -î and -ida, which it soon replaced.

